  "description": "<p>The University of the Pacific is a private Methodist-affiliated university with its main campus in Stockton, California, and graduate campuses in San Francisco and Sacramento. Founded in 1851, it is the oldest chartered University in California.</p><p>Over 6000 students have enrolled at the University of the Pacific annually, with around 470 international students making up the student body.</p><h2>What is the University of the Pacific acceptance rate for International Students?</h2><p>The University of the Pacific acceptance rate is 79%.</p> <p>Students can access helpful resources from the three libraries on its Stockton campus. The William Knox Holt Memorial Library, the Health Sciences Library, and the Arthur A. Dugoni School of Dentistry Library all have a diverse range of books and other materials to refer to for educational and leisurely purposes. The Student Health Center offers medical and mental health services, including counseling and wellness programs. Athletic students are pleased to find many recreational facilities, such as the Alex G. Spanos Center, the Janssen-Lagorio Gymnasium, and the Chris Kjeldsen Pool Complex.</p><p>NCAA Division I sports teams practice and play using these facilities. Multiple research institutes and centers are available on campus, including the Pacific McGeorge Global Center for Business and Development, the Pacific Center for Environmental Studies, and the Brubeck Institute. Similarly, arts programs are supported by various arts facilities, including the Raymond Great Hall, the Faye Spanos Concert Hall, and the Jeannette Powell Art Center. The Stockton campus also has a tall rock-climbing tower.</p> <p><strong>Student Life</strong></p> <p>Famous music artists perform on campus throughout the year. Students run around 150 student clubs that reflect a range of interests, and there are 12 sports clubs to ensure something for everyone. The center for identity and inclusion offers resources and support networks to people from different backgrounds, amplifying the integrative focus of the diverse campus community. The Pacific Tigers compete in NCAA Division I athletics, so many students support their local team by cheering them on at regular games. Introverted students might like to browse the literary selection at the campus bookstore and spend alone time in the library. People from all walks of life find a home at the University of the Pacific.</p>",
